It suffices to show that the polynomials with integer coefficients form a countable set, since $AA$ is then a countable union of finite sets.

For each $d in NN$, let $P_d$ be the set of degree-$d$ polynomials with integer coefficients. The map
$ a_d x^d + a_(d-1) x^(d-1) + ... + a_1 x + a_0 |-> (a_d, a_(d-1), ..., a_1, a_0) $
injects $P_d$ into $ZZ^(d+1)$, which is countable, so each $P_d$ is countable. A countable union of countable sets is countable, so the polynomials with integer coefficients form a countable set, and hence so does $AA$.
